## 3. Spare tempo moves as loopy games

**Definition 3.1 (pass reservoirs).** Each reservoir is a D-game with a single position ω.
- **on_D:** L(ω) = {ω}, R(ω) = ∅. White can pass forever; Black has no move.
- **off_D:** the mirror image.
- **dud_D:** L(ω) = R(ω) = {ω}.

Their CGT analogues are on = {on | }, off = { | off} and dud = {dud | dud} (KNOWN: [WW] ch. 11 "Games
Infinite and Indefinite"; [S] ch. VI). Also KNOWN [S, ch. VI]:
- on exceeds every number;
- on, off and over are stoppers, while dud is not;
- G + dud is a draw for every G in which only running out of moves decides the game.

**Proposition 3.2 (a shuttling king is a pass). PROVED.** Let Z be a king group and z ↔ z′ two non-capturing
White king moves inside F_Z, with:
- in both z and z′, every Black Z-move a suicide in the D-model;
- the two shuttle moves legal in every context, which Lemma 2.2 guarantees, since they stay on F_Z.

Then, in the D-model and for a short context G, r(G ⊞ Z) ≥ r(G ⊞ on_D), with equality if White has no other
Z-moves. (Two shuttles, one for each king: Proposition 3.2′ below.)

*Proof.*
- **21 of 24 agree**: 6/6 Z_off contexts; for Z_on, 6/6 in (W,W), 5/6 in (L,L), 4/6 in (W,L); P9 `Pe2 pf3` gives (W, L) as in P9.
- The 3 disagreements are (V1a)/(V2) failures on this narrow board (the seam makes the e-file 2-3 king steps from the a-file), not failures of the sum theory:
  (i) `Pe2 pe3 pf4`, predicted (L,L), P-model (W,L): White's king sortie Ka5-g4 (not a shuttle move) wins, so P is better for White than D, as Proposition 3.2 allows (r(G ⊞ Z) ≥ r(G ⊞ on_D));
  (ii) `Pe4 pf5`, predicted (W,L), P-model (W,W): after Black's capture f5xe4 the new Black passer is caught by Ka5-g4 (`Ka5 Pb4 Pc5 kc6 pb5 pe4 w` is a White win in P), so Black's decisive event is not a P-win ((V2));
  (iii) `Pe2 Pe3 pe4 pf3 pf4`, predicted (W,L), P-model (L,L): every White move loses; White's capture e3xf4 creates a passer that the D-model scores as a win, but Black's f3xe2 and e1=Q is a faster counter-promotion (a race, (V2)).
- **80 further random contexts** (`chess_rand.py gen 80 31337`, 1-4 pawns on e, f; same oracle, 27 s): 64 of 80 agree. Over all 98 Z_on contexts, 79 agree; 13 differ in White's favour (the P-result is at least the prediction, as the inequality
  r(G ⊞ Z) ≥ r(G ⊞ on_D) of Proposition 3.2 allows: White's non-shuttle king moves); 5 are draws where the D-model predicts a decision (the stalemate mechanism, (V1b); `Pe4 pe5` is one: e4 covers d5 and pe5 is blocked, so Black has no legal move;
  the other four were not diagnosed); 1 is worse for White than predicted ((iii) above, a race, (V2)). Direction table: `logs/chess_direction.txt`.
- Reproduce: `scripts/chess_pred.py gen` (writes `chess/positions_all.txt`-compatible files and `chess/pred.json`), then
  `cc -O2 -o bin/kp_p kings/delegate/out/K7/scripts/draft/kp_p.c -lpthread; bin/kp_p --board cyl:7x6 --pmodel --ep on --promote Q --cap 0 < chess/positions_all.txt` and compare with `chess/pred.json`.
- **Referee status (revised after the K21 referee, 2026-10-27).** The P-model oracle was *not re-run*: the referee built `kp_p` and queued a 12-position sample, but both shared heavy-job slots were held by other jobs for 25 minutes and the run was withdrawn. What was checked:
  the counts above were recounted from K21's own result files (`verify/count_chess.py`: 24 base contexts, 21 agree; 80 random contexts, 64 agree; 79 of 98 Z_on contexts agree; 13 + 5 + 1 differences as listed), and the prediction side was re-derived by hand
  for `Pe2 pf3` ((W, L)), `Pe2 pe3` ((W, W)) and `Pe4 pf5` (predicted (W, L), P-model (W, W)), and the `Pe4 pe5` stalemate explanation is right. So the P-model results stay COMPUTED-1 (one run, not re-run); nothing in the chess part is CHECKED-2.
- **kpsolve (C, cap 1): not obtained.** One cap-1 position (`Ka5 Pb4 Pc5 kc6 pb5 Pe2 pf3 w -`) used more than 55 CPU-seconds when run alone outside the lock (killed at the limit), and a batch of 4 contexts (8 positions, `chess/positions_C.txt`) waited in the
  shared heavy-job queue (both slots held by other kings/ jobs for the whole session) and was withdrawn. So the new contexts are P-model results (the Z_on claims of §5.4 are "PROVED in P"; the chess claim needs (A_Q) at each position), and the only kpsolve (C) value
  for a context here is the one already recorded for P9. Reproduce when the lock is free: `kings/delegate/heavy.sh bin/kp --board cyl:7x6 --ep on --promote QRBN --cap 1 --threads 2 < chess/positions_C.txt`.

**What loopy kings do to the convention.**
- **With a White shuttle (PROVED by Theorem 3.4(b)):** the components' values (numbers, infinitesimals, nimbers)
  are irrelevant except for Black's forcing threats. White cannot be zugzwanged, and Black loses as soon as its
  context tempo runs out.
- **With shuttles on both sides (PROVED in the D-model by Proposition 3.2′; revised after K14 and its referee, 2026-10-27):** for a
  component with four combined king states, each side's shuttle an option and every other option a suicide, all tempo
  content disappears. A position in which neither side can force a decisive event against a passing opponent is a
  draw even where the convention predicts a clear winner, and a side wins iff it can (Theorem 3.4(a), through
  Proposition 3.2′). The chess bridge is Theorem 3.2‴. It needs, per group and context, that every non-shuttle Z-move
  is decisively punished; a win needs this only for the loser and only in the two states in which the winner's king
  stands still. The 7.3 group is **not** of this type: f7–f8=Q is a White Z-move that is no suicide (§5.3). A group of this type
  exists in P on the flat 7×6 board: Z_B of §5.7 has every non-shuttle move losing by force at all 8 roots (PROVED by
  hand, revised after K27 and its referee, 2026-10-27; CHECKED-2 for the computations),
  and beside every context tested its P-result equals r(G ⊞ dud_D) (COMPUTED-1; 225 of 885 contexts untested);
  CONJECTURE that this holds in every context. Whether Z_B is exactly two-shuttle in chess is OPEN (cap 1 gives
  the bounds (W, L); 21 of the 26 deviations lose in chess, CHECKED-2, and five are not settled: §5.7); open: chess
  (cap 2), the cylinder, other boards (§8, item 2). Z_A (§5.7) has one White break.

**Proposition 3.2′ (two shuttling kings are a dud). PROVED (new, K14, 2026-10-27; revised after the K14 referee,
2026-10-27: the D-model part was re-checked by one referee (same model family) and by two brute-force implementations,
on short and loopy G; revised after K34, 2026-10-28: re-derived blind outside the Claude family, every step SOUND, the e.p. and bridge steps under their stated hypotheses).**

*Two-shuttle component.* Let Z be a D-game with four distinct positions (a, b), a ∈ {z, z′}, b ∈ {w, w′}. Write ā for
the other of z, z′ and b̄ for the other of w, w′. In chess z, z′ and w, w′ are the two shuttle squares of the White and
the Black king, and the four positions are the four combined king states. In each of the four positions:
- **(T1)** (ā, b) is a Left option (White's shuttle);
- **(T2)** (a, b̄) is a Right option (Black's shuttle);
- **(T3)** every other Left option is ⊖ (a suicide);
- **(T4)** every other Right option is ⊕ (a suicide).

Z may have further positions. Let G be any D-game (short or not) and x a position of G.
- **(a) Equality.** Under (T1)–(T4), for all four states (a, b) and both sides to move, r(G ⊞ Z) at (x, (a, b)) equals
  r(G ⊞ dud_D) at (x, ω). For short G the value is given by Theorem 3.4(a): a side wins iff it can force a decisive
  event against an opponent who may pass at any time, and otherwise the game is drawn. There is no zugzwang
  (Λ_R ⇒ Λ_L), and a G without terminals is a draw.
- **(b) Further Z-moves.** Under (T1), (T2), (T4) (White may have further Z-moves, of any kind, even to new positions),
  r(G ⊞ Z) ≥ r(G ⊞ dud_D) in the order L < D < W. Under (T1), (T2), (T3), r(G ⊞ Z) ≤ r(G ⊞ dud_D). If both sides have
  further moves there is no comparison in general. The inequalities can be strict (a further White option ⊕ at
  (z, w) makes G = 0 a White win, against D for dud_D).
- **(c) Half-slice.** Suppose only (T2) and (T4) hold, and only at (z, w) and (z, w′), and everything else in Z is
  arbitrary. If r(G ⊞ dud_D) = W at (x, ω) for a side to move s, then r(G ⊞ Z) = W at (x, (z, w)) and at (x, (z, w′))
  for the same s. Mirror: (T1), (T3) at (z, w) and (z′, w) transfer a Black win. So *a win needs only the half of the
  four states in which the winner's king stands still*; the draws, and the exact equality (a), need all four.
- **(d) Each shuttle arrow is needed.** Take G = 0 (one position, no options), where r(G ⊞ dud_D) = (D, D).
  - *Opposition.* Delete the diagonal state (z′, w′) (the kings would touch). Then (z′, w) has no Black shuttle and
    (z, w′) has no White shuttle; their only options are suicides. At (z, w) the pair is (W, L): White to move goes to
    (z′, w) and Black must suicide, Black to move goes to (z, w′) and White must suicide. The first player wins.
  - *One arrow.* Delete Black's shuttle at (z′, w) only: the pair at (z, w) is (W, D).
  - *Every arrow.* Deleting any one of the eight shuttle arrows from an otherwise exact Z changes the result in a
    positive fraction of random instances (COMPUTED-1). At G = 0 each of the eight deletions changes the result at all
    four states: the K14 referee's table and the integrator's own script (`kings/delegate/out/K34/integrator_g0.py`)
    agree, CHECKED-2 (revised after K34, 2026-10-28; K34 reported that only the source state changes, but its script
    tests only that some state changes, and that claim is withdrawn).

*Proof.* Two lemmas, then the parts.

**Lemma A (p-morphism).** Let A, B be D-games and φ a map from the positions of A to those of B (identity on terminals)
such that, for every position u of A and each player s, (i) φ maps every s-option of u to an s-option of φ(u), and
(ii) every s-option of φ(u) is φ of some s-option of u. Then r_A(u, s) = r_B(φ(u), s) for all u, s. A and B may be loopy.
*Proof.* Let U_n^σ be the stage sets of Definition 1.2 (U_0 = ∅; (u, σ) enters at stage n+1 if some σ-option is a
σ-terminal or a position t with (t, σ̄) in U_n^σ; (u, σ̄) enters if every option is a σ-terminal or a t with (t, σ) in
U_n^σ, a position without options entering at stage 1). By induction on n, (u, s) is in U_n^σ(A) iff (φ(u), s) is in
U_n^σ(B). For s = σ, an option of u that is good at stage n maps by (i) to a good option of φ(u), and a good option
of φ(u) is φ of an option of u by (ii), which is good by induction. For s = σ̄, every option of φ(u) is φ of an option
# A8: explicit kings. When does "pawn components + a frozen trébuchet" give the chess result?

Research item A8 ([RESEARCH_PLAN.md](../RESEARCH_PLAN.md), Track A).

The convention behind `cylcgt` (and the 2025 paper):
- it values pawn components under normal play, with a passed pawn worth ±BIG;
- it treats the king group, the trébuchet, as the game 0.

This item puts the kings back and asks when that is right.

## Claim labels

Every claim below carries one label.

- **PROVED**: a proof is written out (in [theory/LOOPY.md](theory/LOOPY.md), or here). One model wrote it.
  An adversarial reviewer has checked it (§7), but no human has yet.
- **CHECKED-2**: computed by two independent implementations that agree, or by our solver and the Lichess
  tablebase.
- **COMPUTED-1**: computed once, by `kings/kpsolve` only. Its correctness rests on the checks in §2.
- **INFERENCE**: our reading of data, not a theorem.

## 1. Answers, in brief

**Is the convention right?** Often not, on small boards.
- Across the three sweeps, 159 of 500 testable components mismatch.
- In 107 of them the kings alone are the cause.
- The other 52 (all on the 7×6 board) are failures of the pawn convention itself: en passant, and passed
  pawns that do not actually win the race.

**Real values with explicit kings.**
- `kings/kpsolve` computes exact win/draw/loss for both sides to move, with real chess rules: kings,
  pawns, en passant, all four promotions, stalemate, and no 50-move rule.
- **CHECKED-2:**
  - It agrees with an independent Python solver on every one of 1,418,480 positions on 3- to 5-file
    boards (§2). This checks the solver; the theory's predictions rest on kpsolve alone (COMPUTED-1, §5).
  - It is consistent with the Lichess tablebase on all 45 positions we could compare: 42 agree exactly, and
    in the other 3 (which need a second promotion) the true value lies inside kpsolve's bounds (§2).

**When the frozen-trébuchet assumption is exact** (Theorem 1.14 and §2 of [theory/LOOPY.md](theory/LOOPY.md)).
The convention gives the chess result when four conditions hold. With (V1)–(V3) the result is the
normal-play result of the pawn components: Theorem 1.14(i), **PROVED** and confirmed by a referee. The step
to cylcgt's BIG arithmetic, 1.14(ii), needs the corrected (V4) below (§7). The conditions:
- **(V1)** the kings never interact with the pawn components, nobody is stalemated, and en passant obeys
  condition (E) of L1.6 in `notes/lemmas.md` (Theorem 1.14 states (E) inside (V1)(a));
- **(V2)** every "decisive event" (a new passed pawn) really wins;
- **(V3)** every move inside the king group is decisively punished;
- **(V4)** no two components carry passed-pawn threats of opposite sign (counting a passer created by
  *either* side's move, a correction from the review in §7), and BIG exceeds every tempo count.

(V3) has a checkable local certificate: the punisher promotes within T moves even if the defender may
pass, and the defender cannot promote anywhere in T moves (Theorem 2.7).
- **Theorem 2.7 is PROVED after repair, in the promotion model P** (task K7, 2026-10-22). P is chess in which
  the first promotion wins. The bound μ ≥ T + 1 on the defender's counter-promotion is exact there.
- **It does not give a chess win, and no margin on μ does.** Examples on the 7×6 cylinder, each a Black win in
  P (kpsolve, cap 2):
  - `Kb4 ka1 Pe4 pa2` with Kb4–c5 meets the horizon-1 certificate, yet the chess result is a draw;
  - `Kc5 ka3 Pe2 pc4` with Kc5–d5 meets the horizon-2 certificate, yet White wins in chess.
- A chess conclusion therefore needs the per-position check (A_Q).
- See LOOPY.md, "What Theorem 2.7 does not give". The repaired proof was checked only by AI reviewers.
- With **T = 1**, condition (L2) of the certificate holds in every chess-legal context without a passer, on
  boards with at least 5 ranks, provided the defender has no king-group pawn one move from promotion
  (Proposition 2.10a). (L1) must still be checked for the group, and (L3) in each context.
  (Wording corrected after the main project's T30 proof audit, `notes/proof_audit2/REPORT.md`: the earlier
  sentence claimed the whole certificate.)
- With **T ≥ 2**, (L2) fails in some context on boards at least |F_Z| + 4 files wide (Proposition 2.10b),
  so the zero is not certified in every context. That it is actually context-dependent is a CONJECTURE, supported by P4.
- The real trébuchets have T of about 5–7. Their zero is therefore certified only beside slow or locked
  components.

**Spare king tempo moves.** A king that can shuttle forever without loss is not 0 but a loopy game:

